## Solution

The Validation Scalar for a permutation $(a_1, \ldots, a_{20})$ is

$$V = a_1 - a_2 + a_3 - a_4 + \cdots + a_{19} - a_{20} = \sum_{i\text{ odd}} a_i \;-\; \sum_{i\text{ even}} a_i.$$

**Step 1: Reduce to a subset-sum problem.** Let $S = \{a_1, a_3, a_5, \ldots, a_{19}\}$ be the set of 10 numbers placed in odd positions. The 10 even positions receive the complement $\{1,\ldots,20\}\setminus S$. The value of $V$ depends only on $S$, not on the order within the odd/even positions, since

$$V = \sum_{s \in S} s \;-\; \sum_{s \notin S} s \;=\; 2\sum_{s \in S} s \;-\; \sum_{k=1}^{20} k \;=\; 2\sum_{s \in S} s \;-\; 210.$$

The map $S \mapsto 2\sum_{s\in S}s - 210$ is a bijection between possible subset-sums and possible scalars. Hence the number of distinct values of $V$ equals the number of distinct sums of 10-element subsets of $\{1,2,\ldots,20\}$.

**Step 2: Determine the range of 10-subset sums.** The minimum is achieved by $\{1,2,\ldots,10\}$:

$$\sum_{k=1}^{10} k = 55.$$

The maximum is achieved by $\{11,12,\ldots,20\}$:

$$\sum_{k=11}^{20} k = 210 - 55 = 155.$$

**Step 3: Show every integer in $[55, 155]$ is attainable (contiguity).** We prove that for any 10-subset $S \subseteq \{1,\ldots,20\}$ with sum $s < 155$, there exists a 10-subset with sum $s+1$.

Suppose $S \neq \{11,12,\ldots,20\}$. We claim there exists $a \in S$ with $a < 20$ and $a+1 \notin S$. Suppose not; then for every $a \in S$ with $a < 20$, we have $a+1 \in S$. This means $S$ is "upward closed": starting from its smallest element $j$, the set must contain $j, j+1, j+2, \ldots, 20$, forcing $S = \{j, j+1, \ldots, 20\}$ with $|S| = 10$, i.e., $S = \{11,\ldots,20\}$ — contradicting our assumption.

Therefore such an $a$ exists. Replacing $a$ by $a+1$ yields another 10-element subset $S' = (S \setminus \{a\}) \cup \{a+1\}$ with $\sum S' = s + 1$.

By induction starting from $\{1,\ldots,10\}$ (sum 55), every integer from 55 to 155 is attainable as a 10-subset sum.

**Step 4: Count.** The attainable sums are exactly the integers $55, 56, \ldots, 155$, giving

$$155 - 55 + 1 = 101$$

distinct sums, and hence **101 distinct values** of the Validation Scalar.

(Equivalently, $V$ ranges over $2\cdot 55 - 210 = -100$ to $2\cdot 155 - 210 = 100$, i.e., all 101 even... in fact all integers from $-100$ to $100$ step $2$... no: since the sum takes all 101 consecutive integer values, $V = 2\cdot(\text{sum}) - 210$ takes all 101 consecutive even... — precisely the 101 values $-100, -98, \ldots, 98, 100$.)

The number of different values the Validation Scalar can take is $\boxed{101}$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
